      <section class="article-section" id="unpaid-weeks">
        <h2>What Unpaid Weeks Cost</h2>
        <p>The $135,200.00 headline assumes 52 paid weeks. Unpaid time off, whether that is vacation without PTO, a seasonal gap, or a slow January, comes straight off the year. The take-home cost is bigger than the gross cost looks, because the weeks you lose are full-rate weeks:</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="pay-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Paid weeks</th><th>Hours paid</th><th>Annual gross</th><th>Federal-only take-home</th><th>Take-home lost</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>52 (no unpaid time)</strong></td><td>2,080</td><td><strong>$135,200.00</strong></td><td><strong>$103,675.20</strong></td><td>—</td></tr>
              <tr><td>51 (1 unpaid week)</td><td>2,040</td><td>$132,600.00</td><td>$101,898.10</td><td>$1,777.10</td></tr>
              <tr><td>50 (2 unpaid weeks)</td><td>2,000</td><td>$130,000.00</td><td>$100,121.00</td><td>$3,554.20</td></tr>
              <tr><td>48 (4 unpaid weeks)</td><td>1,920</td><td>$124,800.00</td><td>$96,566.80</td><td>$7,108.40</td></tr>
              <tr><td>39 (13 unpaid weeks)</td><td>1,560</td><td>$101,400.00</td><td>$80,164.90</td><td>$23,510.30</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>One unpaid week costs $2,600.00 gross and $1,777.10 of take-home. That is more than a $1-an-hour raise is worth ($1,421.68), which is the honest way to weigh a job with unpaid gaps against one with paid time off. And the rung drop that started at $56 is still exactly one step wide: one unpaid week at $65 ($101,898.10) falls $355.42 short of a full-time $64-an-hour year, while at $64 the same week fell only $328.08 short of a full-time $63-an-hour year, yet it still beats a full-time $63-an-hour year by $1,066.26. Two unpaid weeks cost $5,200.00 gross and $3,554.20 of take-home, and they land exactly on a $130,000 salary’s take-home ($100,121.00), though they fall $710.84 short of a full-time $63-an-hour year and beat a full-time $62-an-hour year by the same $710.84. Four unpaid weeks cost $10,400.00 gross and $7,108.40 of take-home, landing exactly on a full-time $60-an-hour year ($96,566.80, to the cent), yet beating a full-time $59-an-hour year by $1,421.68. Thirteen unpaid weeks are the real flip on this page: $80,164.90 of take-home beats a $100,000 salary by $984.90. At $64, thirteen unpaid weeks trailed it by $112.56, so the gap has swung by $1,097.46 in one rung, and $65 is the first rate on this ladder where missing a full quarter of the year still clears six figures. The near-misses are worth a look as well: one unpaid week at $65 still beats a full-time $60-an-hour year by $5,331.30, so the first unpaid week only drops you one rung, and thirteen unpaid weeks land on exactly the same $101,400.00 gross as a 30-hour week worked all year, which is why the schedules table and this table agree to the cent. Our <a href="/articles/how-much-is-64-an-hour-a-year/">$64-an-hour guide</a> and <a href="/articles/how-much-is-63-an-hour-a-year/">$63-an-hour guide</a> price the same unpaid-week steps at their rates, so the three guides read as one ladder.</p>
      </section>

